1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:07,884
Can you show me recover a deleted branch?

2
00:00:08,433 --> 00:00:16,681
Deleting a branch deletes a pointer; the commits remain until garbage collection removes unreachable objects (weeks,

3
00:00:16,681 --> 00:00:17,534
by default).

4
00:00:17,534 --> 00:00:20,307
We recover deleted branches three ways:

5
00:00:20,307 --> 00:00:26,208
from the reflog, from another clone, and with git fsck when the reflog has nothing.

6
00:00:27,967 --> 00:00:32,366
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

7
00:00:32,900 --> 00:00:40,277
Ada works on a branch, pushes it, then deletes it locally and on the server (thinking it was merged):

8
00:00:40,277 --> 00:00:40,715
Way 1:

9
00:00:40,715 --> 00:00:41,519
the reflog.

10
00:00:41,519 --> 00:00:46,705
Ada had the branch checked out, so HEAD's reflog knows its last commit:

11
00:00:47,267 --> 00:00:47,805
Way 1:

12
00:00:47,805 --> 00:00:48,793
the reflog.

13
00:00:48,793 --> 00:00:55,166
Ada had the branch checked out, so HEAD's reflog knows its last commit:

14
00:00:55,166 --> 00:00:55,704
Way 2:

15
00:00:55,704 --> 00:00:56,961
another clone.

16
00:00:57,500 --> 00:00:57,968
Way 2:

17
00:00:57,968 --> 00:00:59,061
another clone.

18
00:00:59,061 --> 00:01:06,084
Grace had fetched the branch before it was deleted; her origin/feature-hours still has it,

19
00:01:06,084 --> 00:01:08,035
and she can push it back:

20
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:11,276
Let me try something.

21
00:01:11,276 --> 00:01:16,760
Grace reviewed a branch of Ada's that she never checked out (only fetched it).

22
00:01:17,300 --> 00:01:19,449
Grace's reflog has no entry:

23
00:01:19,449 --> 00:01:27,199
her HEAD was never on that branch, and the reflog of origin/review-me was deleted with the reference.

24
00:01:27,199 --> 00:01:31,956
The commit is still in her object store, though, unreferenced.

25
00:01:31,956 --> 00:01:34,105
git fsck finds such commits:

26
00:01:35,867 --> 00:01:37,666
Here is the fix.

27
00:01:39,400 --> 00:01:40,113
Got it.

28
00:01:40,113 --> 00:01:42,865
What do I need to remember?

29
00:01:43,400 --> 00:01:48,755
A deleted branch is a deleted pointer; the commits stay until garbage collection.

30
00:01:49,300 --> 00:01:56,865
Reflog (if you were on it) → another clone or the PR → git fsck --unreachable as a last resort.

31
00:01:57,400 --> 00:02:02,360
git branch NAME SHA recreates it; push it back if it lived on the server.

32
00:02:04,100 --> 00:02:07,797
I keep hearing about recover a deleted commit.

33
00:02:07,797 --> 00:02:09,325
What is it, really?

34
00:02:09,867 --> 00:02:13,388
A commit can disappear from a branch in many ways:

35
00:02:13,388 --> 00:02:18,739
an interactive rebase that dropped it, an --amend that replaced it, a reset.

36
00:02:18,739 --> 00:02:23,739
We recover one with the reflog and cherry-pick, and then see the limit:

37
00:02:23,739 --> 00:02:27,894
once the reflog has expired and garbage collection has run,

38
00:02:27,894 --> 00:02:31,626
only another copy (the server, a colleague) can help.

39
00:02:33,367 --> 00:02:37,766
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

40
00:02:38,300 --> 00:02:40,937
A cleanup rebase drops the wrong line:

41
00:02:40,937 --> 00:02:43,574
"Add mocha" instead of something else.

42
00:02:43,574 --> 00:02:48,709
The reflog lists every commit HEAD has been on, including the dropped one:

43
00:02:49,267 --> 00:02:54,053
The reflog lists every commit HEAD has been on, including the dropped one:

44
00:02:54,053 --> 00:02:57,351
Bring its change back on top of the current branch:

45
00:02:57,900 --> 00:03:01,470
Bring its change back on top of the current branch:

46
00:03:03,233 --> 00:03:04,718
Let me try something.

47
00:03:04,718 --> 00:03:11,648
Ada pushes her work, then "starts over" on the chai commit and cleans the repository aggressively:

48
00:03:12,200 --> 00:03:18,021
The reflog is empty and gc --prune=now deleted every unreachable object.

49
00:03:18,021 --> 00:03:20,690
Locally, the chai commit is gone:

50
00:03:22,433 --> 00:03:26,865
Had the commit never been pushed, it would be lost for good:

51
00:03:26,865 --> 00:03:29,228
push work you care about, early.

52
00:03:30,967 --> 00:03:31,680
Got it.

53
00:03:31,680 --> 00:03:34,431
What do I need to remember?

54
00:03:34,967 --> 00:03:39,142
Dropped, amended or reset commits stay in the reflog:

55
00:03:39,142 --> 00:03:42,056
find them, cherry-pick or reset back.

56
00:03:42,600 --> 00:03:47,874
reflog expire + gc --prune=now make them unrecoverable locally.

57
00:03:48,433 --> 00:03:51,276
Then only other copies help:

58
00:03:51,276 --> 00:03:54,525
the server, colleagues, PR refs.

59
00:03:54,525 --> 00:03:56,048
Push your work.

60
00:03:57,800 --> 00:04:00,970
How does recover after a hard reset work?

61
00:04:01,533 --> 00:04:08,306
git reset --hard is the most feared command, but what it destroys depends on how far your work got:

62
00:04:08,306 --> 00:04:15,558
committed work is fully recoverable, staged work is recoverable with some digging (git fsck --lost-found),

63
00:04:15,558 --> 00:04:18,157
and only unstaged work is really gone.

64
00:04:18,157 --> 00:04:20,278
We recover each case carefully.

65
00:04:22,033 --> 00:04:26,433
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

66
00:04:26,967 --> 00:04:28,164
Committed work.

67
00:04:28,164 --> 00:04:30,240
Reset two commits too far:

68
00:04:30,240 --> 00:04:31,837
Check before acting:

69
00:04:31,837 --> 00:04:34,711
what was the state before the reset?

70
00:04:35,267 --> 00:04:36,776
Check before acting:

71
00:04:36,776 --> 00:04:39,493
what was the state before the reset?

72
00:04:39,493 --> 00:04:41,832
ORIG_HEAD and the reflog agree:

73
00:04:41,832 --> 00:04:46,586
Recover by moving the branch back (nothing else changed since):

74
00:04:47,133 --> 00:04:51,833
Recover by moving the branch back (nothing else changed since):

75
00:04:53,567 --> 00:04:55,220
Let me try something.

76
00:04:55,220 --> 00:05:00,021
Work was staged but not committed, and a hard reset wipes it:

77
00:05:00,567 --> 00:05:03,917
There is no commit, so the reflog has nothing.

78
00:05:03,917 --> 00:05:11,639
But git add wrote the file's content as a blob into the object store; the reset removed it from the index,

79
00:05:11,639 --> 00:05:15,281
leaving the blob dangling (no name, no reference):

80
00:05:17,033 --> 00:05:22,491
--lost-found copied the dangling objects to .git/lost-found/other/.

81
00:05:22,491 --> 00:05:26,238
Find the one with your content and restore it:

82
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:28,713
Got it.

83
00:05:28,713 --> 00:05:31,464
What do I need to remember?

84
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:35,572
Committed work survives reset --hard:

85
00:05:35,572 --> 00:05:37,309
ORIG_HEAD, reflog.

86
00:05:37,867 --> 00:05:41,351
Staged work survives as dangling blobs:

87
00:05:41,351 --> 00:05:43,316
git fsck --lost-found.

88
00:05:43,867 --> 00:05:46,308
Unstaged work does not survive:

89
00:05:46,308 --> 00:05:49,301
commit or stash before risky commands.
